    {
      "@id": "concept:c2018c00199-sch-2-sec-16ab",
      "@type": "cml:Concept",
      "skos:prefLabel": "16AB Matters relating to victim impact statements",
      "cml:realizedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/c2018c00199#sch-2-sec-16ab"
      },
      "cml:curationStatus": {
        "@id": "cml:Proposed"
      },
      "cml:schemaVersion": "2",
      "cml:proposedBy": {
        "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/tools/legis2cml"
      },
      "prov:wasDerivedFrom": {
        "@id": "c2018c00199.pdf"
      },
      "dc:source": "c2018c00199.pdf, p.30",
      "values:originalText": "(1) This section applies in relation to victim impact statements made\nknown to a court as described in paragraph 16A(2)(ea).\n\n(2) Only one victim impact statement may be made for each victim of\nan offence, unless the court gives leave.\n\n(3) No implication is to be drawn from the absence of a victim impact\nstatement for a victim.\n\n(4) All or part of a victim impact statement for a victim may be read to\nthe court by or on behalf of the victim.\n\n(5) A victim impact statement is not to be read to the court, or\notherwise taken into account, to the extent that:\n\n(a) it expresses an opinion about an appropriate sentence; or\n\n(b) it is offensive, threatening, intimidating or harassing; or\n\n(c) admitting it into evidence would otherwise not be in the\ninterests of justice.\n\n(6) The person convicted of the offence may only test the facts in a\nvictim impact statement:\n\n(a) by way of cross-examining the maker of the statement; and\n\n(b) if the court gives leave to do so.\n\n(7) For the purposes of Part IAD (about protecting vulnerable\npersons):\n\n(a) giving evidence includes giving a reading under\nsubsection (4); and\n\n(b) a cross-examination includes a cross-examination under\nsubsection (6).\nNote:\nThis confirms that any protections available under Part IAD will be\navailable for the reading or the cross-examination.\n\nSupporting victims and witnesses in criminal proceedings  Schedule 2\nApplication of amendments  Part 3\n\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (Law Enforcement Integrity, Vulnerable Witness\nProtection and Other Measures) Act 2013\nCompilation date: 23/5/18\nRegistered: 30/5/18",
      "skos:definition": "(1) This section applies in relation to victim impact statements made\nknown to a court as described in paragraph 16A(2)(ea).\n\n(2) Only one victim impact statement may be made for each victim of\nan offence, unless the court gives leave.\n\n(3) No implication is to be drawn from the absence of a victim impact\nstatement for a victim.\n\n(4) All or part of a victim impact statement for a victim may be read to\nthe court by or on behalf of the victim.\n\n(5) A victim impact statement is not to be read to the court, or\notherwise taken into account, to the extent that:\n\n(a) it expresses an opinion about an appropriate sentence; or\n\n(b) it is offensive, threatening, intimidating or harassing; or\n\n(c) admitting it into evidence would otherwise not be in the\ninterests of justice.\n\n(6) The person convicted of the offence may only test the facts in a\nvictim impact statement:\n\n(a) by way of cross-examining the maker of the statement; and\n\n(b) if the court gives leave to do so.\n\n(7) For the purposes of Part IAD (about protecting vulnerable\npersons):\n\n(a) giving evidence includes giving a reading under\nsubsection (4); and\n\n(b) a cross-examination includes a cross-examination under\nsubsection (6).\nNote:\nThis confirms that any protections available under Part IAD will be\navailable for the reading or the cross-examination.\n\nSupporting victims and witnesses in criminal proceedings  Schedule 2\nApplication of amendments  Part 3\n\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (Law Enforcement Integrity, Vulnerable Witness\nProtection and Other Measures) Act 2013\nCompilation date: 23/5/18\nRegistered: 30/5/18",
      "cml:hasPart": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2018c00199-sch-2-sec-16ab-ss-1"
        },
