      },
      "dc:source": "c2018c00199.pdf, p.50",
      "values:originalText": "Insert:\nInternational Residual Mechanism for Criminal Tribunals:\n\n(a) means the International Residual Mechanism for Criminal\nTribunals, established by United Nations Security Council\nResolution 1966 S/RES/1966 (2010); and\n\n(b) includes the organs referred to in Article 4 of the Statute of\nthe Tribunal.\nNote 1:\nIn 2013, the text of United Nations Security Council resolutions was\naccessible through the United Nations website (www.un.org).\nNote 2:\nThe United Nations Security Council decided on 22 December 2010\nto establish this Mechanism to carry out residual functions of the\nFormer Yugoslavia Tribunal and the Rwanda Tribunal.\n\nAssisting the International Residual Mechanism for Criminal Tribunals  Schedule 5\n\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (Law Enforcement Integrity, Vulnerable Witness\nProtection and Other Measures) Act 2013\nCompilation date: 23/5/18\nRegistered: 30/5/18",
      "skos:definition": "Insert:\nInternational Residual Mechanism for Criminal Tribunals:\n\n(a) means the International Residual Mechanism for Criminal\nTribunals, established by United Nations Security Council\nResolution 1966 S/RES/1966 (2010); and\n\n(b) includes the organs referred to in Article 4 of the Statute of\nthe Tribunal.\nNote 1:\nIn 2013, the text of United Nations Security Council resolutions was\naccessible through the United Nations website (www.un.org).\nNote 2:\nThe United Nations Security Council decided on 22 December 2010\nto establish this Mechanism to carry out residual functions of the\nFormer Yugoslavia Tribunal and the Rwanda Tribunal.\n\nAssisting the International Residual Mechanism for Criminal Tribunals  Schedule 5\n\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (Law Enforcement Integrity, Vulnerable Witness\nProtection and Other Measures) Act 2013\nCompilation date: 23/5/18\nRegistered: 30/5/18",
      "cml:asserts": [
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2018c00199-sch-5-sec-2-norm"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2018c00199-sch-5-sec-2-logic-descriptionlogic-1"
        },
        {
          "@id": "concept:c2018c00199-sch-5-sec-2-logic-descriptionlogic-2"
        }
      ],
      "cml:integrityHash": "aa2110f06a770a1dc8605f053c6066308c752e20368482804f1580f272b4c2d2",
      "skos:note": "Defines International Residual Mechanism for Criminal Tribunals"
    },
    {
      "@id": "https://ns.webcivics.net/values/c2018c00199#sch-5-sec-2",
      "@type": "cof:Section",
      "cof:title": "2 Subsection 4(1)",
      "values:kind": "section",
      "cof:pageNumber": 50,
      "values:originalText": "Insert:\nInternational Residual Mechanism for Criminal Tribunals:\n\n(a) means the International Residual Mechanism for Criminal\nTribunals, established by United Nations Security Council\nResolution 1966 S/RES/1966 (2010); and\n\n(b) includes the organs referred to in Article 4 of the Statute of\nthe Tribunal.\nNote 1:\nIn 2013, the text of United Nations Security Council resolutions was\naccessible through the United Nations website (www.un.org).\nNote 2:\nThe United Nations Security Council decided on 22 December 2010\nto establish this Mechanism to carry out residual functions of the\nFormer Yugoslavia Tribunal and the Rwanda Tribunal.\n\nAssisting the International Residual Mechanism for Criminal Tribunals  Schedule 5\n\nCrimes Legislation Amendment (Law Enforcement Integrity, Vulnerable Witness\nProtection and Other Measures) Act 2013\nCompilation date: 23/5/18\nRegistered: 30/5/18"
    },
